<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This suffers from a range of problems. I have highlighted a lot of examples of just, not good writing: I recommend grabbing something like LibreOffice Writer or Grammarly and running this through it to try and fix up a lot of the grammar.</p>
<p>Your ConProcs and Description are both big blocks of text that need broken up more. In particular, your ConProcs feel overly detailed and introduce a range of things that aren't referenced again (e.g. it being tied up, inspections, armed guards, etc), and it feels like it could be streamlined down to stuff that's either interesting (e.g. details of it replicating appendages) or a hook for later material.</p>
<p>The core problem with a lot of the rest is that the flow of information is just&#8230;not very good? You have a discovery section and two addendums that don't really add a tonne beyond vaguely referencing the core horror here; followed by a relatively bland re-telling of something horrifying which really feels like it needs to be like&#8230;something happening in real time, y'know?</p>
<p>This is rounded out with a note that spells out uh what feels like it should be everything but doesn't actually convey anything beyond what the discovery already implied and that it has extra magical powers that the foundation knows about&#8230;somehow. Iunno: this note feels excessively like having a character just stand there and go 'oh yes this thing is really dangerous super risky, if it got too big, it could destroy everything' instead of like&#8230;establishing that? By showing it?</p>
<p>If I am to be extremely real with this: in the current state I would downvote this and I would not expect it to survive. This is really going to need a lot of work to get into a serviceable state.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX shown and hung before containment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like 'SCP-XXXX before being contained.' would be clearer; really it's the 'shown and hung' that feels super awkward to me; and I'm trying to avoid having to handle that. Picking one of the two would likely work too.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX resides in a compact box consisting of concrete</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'resides' feels very passive; like it's just kinda in there for whatever reason; whereas something like 'is contained' sets up more of a feeling of it being a deliberate choice.</p>
<p>'consisting of' is weird here; it's technically correct I guess but something like 'made out of' or 'constructed from' would be clearer.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>set on a table in a soundproof room in site-19.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>why is it on a table? Also site names are capitalised (i.e. &quot;Site-19&quot;).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Scp-XXXX is to be tied up with any sort of bonded material such as fiber or elastic rope when not in examination.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>SCP is always capitalised; which is something you've not done a few times here.</p>
<p>What does 'tied up' mean here? Like, hung up like a shower curtain? Restrained as if it was alive? Why does it have to be done with a bonded material?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Research and inspection checks are to be made when SCP-XXXX has consumed enough matter to increase or exceed above its average size</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This comes across a little weird because it feels like you should establish it has material to consume before mentioning it's consumed matter; or mention that it's not to be allowed to consume matter first.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>or when recent developments <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">and info</span> have been made regarding SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'into SCP-XXXX' would likely feel less awkward than 'have been made regarding SCP-XXXX' here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Only researchers with clearance level 3 or higher are allowed within the <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">confinement of the</span> anomaly’s cell.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>During an inspection, standard earplugs, as well as protective equipment: such as class-A hazmat suits, must be worn during the process.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is just, awkward. Consider: &quot;During an inspection, protective equipment including earplugs and Class-A hazmat suits must be worn.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If SCP-XXXX is seen or noted replicating appendages, limbs; or any extremities that were once part of SCP-XXXX-A such as eyes, arms, veins, hair or legs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You open this sentence with an 'If', follow it with a big list of conditions, and then end it without saying what happens if the conditions are true. I would probably trim this down a lot: appendages and limbs are roughly synonymous; and 'eyes' and 'veins' aren't extremities.</p>
<p>Consider: &quot;If SCP-XXXX replicates appendages or any body parts that were once part of SCP-XXXX-A, then&#8230;&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>With the addition of two armed guards to be stationed outside of the containment chamber until SCP-XXXX is starved enough were re-containment inside SCP-XXXX’s box is plausible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a weird sentence fragment that feels like it's meant to be part of the previous sentence but was broken up cos it'd be super long if you didn't. Perhaps 'During the lockdown, two armed guards are to be&#8230;.'</p>
<p>'plausible' would be better replaced with 'possible'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX's extra exterminates will deteriorate over a period of time without the consistency of sustaining itself.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I believe you mean 'extremities' and not 'exterminates' here?</p>
<p>'without the consistency of sustaining itself' is not a phrase that I can parse. I believe something like &quot;without the provision of matter to sustain it&quot; would work better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX can manipulate and move these limbs seemingly with apparent ease. Scp-XXXX’s main goal seems to consist of blending in as seamlessly as possible with the former exception of any or all extra extremities that can break the illusion of Scp-XXXX’s disguise.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Both of these sentences would be better suited to the description.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is a 3&#160;m x 1.5&#160;m white shower curtain</p>
</blockquote>
<p>On the one hand, this is a really big, weirdly proportioned shower curtain; on the other, it kinda feels like the size doesn't matter?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and bears signs of considerable use, molding, and age</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like 'considerable use' and 'age' are kinda&#8230;synonymous?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subjects within a 10&#160;m radius of SCP-XXXX that hear the whispers undergo a hypnotic and frenzied state.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the moment in the draft where my doubts go from 'this is a little janky but with some changes, it could work' to 'big concern that this is just not an interesting thing'.</p>
<p>The whispers feel like such a weird cliche and it's coupled with a) rage state b) compulsion effect that it just feels here like this is a bad coldpost. And it's a shame because the idea of the curtain absorbing people is great and this just feels entirely unnecessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Footnote 1: It is currently observed that animals such as: Domesticated dogs, cats, and even cases of un-domesticated wolves, deer, and bears are unaffected by SCP-XXXX’s anomaly’s effects. It is unknown if this applies to all types of animals including sets of particular species or just the ones listed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is wack. Just, really confusing list: I think you're wanting a semi-colon after wolves to separate 'and even undomesticated wolves' from 'deer and bears are unaffected' but really it doesn't feel like any of this detail is important or interesting.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While the appendage or limb that’s stuck is to be amputated immediately.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is another one of those sentences that reads like it was meant to be part of the last sentence and then you just shoved a period into a random place to split it up so it wasn't too long.</p>
<p>'that's stuck' also feels really casual here; perhaps 'that is attached to SCP-XXXX is to be amputated&#8230;' would work better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Salvaging as much as possible off of SCP-XXXX to avoid the further feeding and development of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yet another weird sentence that feels cut off the last sentence. I'm gonna stop highlighting this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After a period of one (1) to three (3) days</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;one (1)&quot; notation is only really done in cases where precision is really important (generally dispensing pharmaceuticals) and I would advise basically never doing it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>from contact (or died from natural causes) The following anomalies' effects will begin to take place.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>random mid sentence capital</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Rapidly succumbing to blood loss shortly after.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>sentence fragment</p>
<blockquote>
<p>will start to give off a<span style="color: green">n</span> unusual odor, consistent with the subject's last eaten meal. Or <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">In</span><span style="color: green">in</span> rare cases<span style="color: green,they## may give off an odor consistent --too--##green">with</span> ████.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This redaction sucks. Anyone who is cleared to know about this SCP needs to know what odours to expect here so they can make sure there's not a containment breach; and from an out-of-universe position, anything you put here is likely a lot more interesting than a box that says &quot;*shrugs* iunno&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-A will become aggressive and irritable</p>
</blockquote>
<p>why?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">enough</span> human matter equivalent to SCP-XXXX’s current size and weight is consumed within a recorded one (1) to two (2) day period</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'equivalent' feels janky here, maybe 'equal to' would be better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When in this state, SCP-XXXX can enhance all senses which include: smell, hearing, vision, taste, and touch.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>how do they know this?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX seems to be able to manipulate and move these limbs around itself to better blend in and close distance between itself and its target.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'seems to'? It either has done this and can or hasn't done this and can't, it doesn't seem like there's much room for uncertainty.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Champagne Illinois on 7/27/2005 (Stated below).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm using this to say that a table for these addendums is a bad choice. I would probably label the first bit 'Discovery' and then having the rest as standalone headers in the style of &quot;<strong>Description:</strong> words here&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to a three day period of silence designated from 7/27/2005 - 7/30/2005</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'designated' is really weirdly shoved in here and can easily be cut.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>225 reports of civilian deaths were filled by Champagne Illinois to the Foundation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>do you mean 'filed'? Why is a town filing them to the Foundation like the Foundation is a secret organisation?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As an immediate response to this unusual jump and spike of numbers. The Foundation took interest and shut out every entrance into this town, making it a priority that no one enters or leaves the surrounding area.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>yet another awkward sentence break, though this time you leave the start awkward, not the end.</p>
<p>I am going to stop for real this time: your writing is <em>littered</em> with poor sentence structure like this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(Commander Linwood proceeds to slam his hands on the table, standing up in anger.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd use italics (<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">//italicised&#32;text//</span> gives <em>italicised text</em>) to make these feel more distinct from the speech.</p>
<p>Stuff like this could be done between dialogue lines too for more emphasis:</p>
<div class="blockquote">
<p><strong>Dr. Connor:</strong> Do you know why the wouldn't stop screaming, commander?</p>
<p><em>(Commanded Linwood proceeds to slam his hands on the table and stand up in anger.)</em></p>
<p><strong>MTF Epsilon-6 commander Linwood:</strong> I don’t fucking know why. I’m still hear aren't I!? If I stuck around trying to help and console my men, I wouldn’t be alive, talking to you.</p>
</div>
<p>I'd also shorten Linwood's title here to just e.g. Linwood or Cmdr Linwood</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Closing Remarks from doctor conner:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Both 'doctor' and 'conner' should be capitalised here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX has shown complete sentience beyond previous thought</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'previously'? this is hard to parse</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it posses several other anomalies effects that are not able to be used because it remains here on earth</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'anomalous'</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
</div>
</div>

