			<div class="content" id="post-content-4837790">
									

<p><em>noting a PM request to take another look</em></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The quality of the english within this is still subpar. I have attempted to note as much as I can but it is extremely plausible that I have missed a lot and you're likely going to have to try and refine it more yourself, or get additional help.</p>
<p>The actual narrative arc here is&#8230;eh. There's a theoretically interesting story of the SCP taking someone over, secretly gaining access to the facility, causing a containment breach and like doing stuff but this doesn't execute it well at all. There are, depending on whether you count the two incident addenda as introduction to this story or not (which I personally wouldn't since it's not clear that they're introducing this story until after it happens) about 800-1100 words before this story even begins, which is not ideal.</p>
<p>Then, a lot of the story just feels very&#8230;generic or elided: there's an interview log that doesn't really do anything new with the concept or introduce the characters really or even convey new information so it just feels very much like it's there; and then it's followed by a short record of the foundation trying to terminate this thing that's just kinda there; and then it's followed by a bunch of stuff after the story ends that kinda hints at stuff happening but not in much depth and it feels&#8230;underwhelming? Or, rather, it feels like all the interesting stuff happens either between the two interviews or after the second interview and none of it is shown to a reader.</p>
<p>Like, the SCP causes a containment breach for unclear reasons: presumably to escape? But it's not really clear what it's trying to do beyond a generic 'return to where it came from' so it's very whatever. And then it's recaptured&#8230;somehow? Like it's grand plan comes crashing down but we never see any part of the plan or how it was foiled so it's like whatever.</p>
<p>This really needs focus: at the end of the day, what do you expect a reader to get out of this? What's the core running story throughout it? And, in turn, how can you bring that story into focus and give it the words it needs to work?</p>
<p>If I was to be extremely honest: I would take this back to the conceptual stage and try and get some reviewers to help you pace this story. Figure out what the beginning, middle and end are, figure out how you can get a reader through the key plot points; figure out how you can evoke the dread that this feels like it's meant to in a reader.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is to be tied up with any sort of bonded material such as fiber or elastic rope when not in an examination, as to restrict possible movement when removed for examination and provide ease of access to SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Uh this feels like you've added a bunch of stuff whilst editing and then forgotten to remove the end of the sentence?</p>
<p>'tied up' feels very casual here too and I'm really not sure what the best way around it is other than cutting this whole section and letting the box handle the containment?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Research and inspection checks are to be made when SCP-XXXX consumes enough matter to increase or exceed above its average size</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'increase or exceed above its average size' is needlessly confusing: 'grow above its average size' might be clearer though it feels like this should be tied to a fixed size rather than an average since 'average' implies natural variation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>or when recent developments into SCP-XXXX have been discovered.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'developments' and 'discovered' together here feels really awkward: 'made' would be better than 'discovered' I think but it also feels like it needs specificity about what the developments are in</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX should never be removed from its containment for any other reason than testing or research purposes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>would read better with 'other' after 'reason'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>until SCP-XXXX is starved enough<span style="text-decoration: line-through;">, where</span><span style="color: green">that</span> re-containment inside SCP-XXXX’s box is possible</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>If SCP-XXXX comes into contact with skin or any exposed part of the body; the person in question who has made initial contact with SCP-XXXX is to be marked as SCP-XXXX-A.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The flow of this is wrong: you have to explain what happens with someone who makes contact with the SCP before you can say why they are given a SCP designation.</p>
<p>This kinda implies that the anomaly is marking them as -A or that it's in some way tied into their identity as a result of the anomaly; when really it's the foundation designating them as a sub-anomaly because of the anomalous stuff which happens to them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The appendage or limb that’s attached to SCP-XXXX is to be amputated immediately, as to avoid the further feeding and development of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Consider:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The appendage attached to SCP-XXXX is to be amputated immediately to prevent further development of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not a fan of 'development' here but I'm not sure what's better: perhaps 'adaption by SCP-XXXX' or something longer that actually covers what change happens?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>then an immediate termination of SCP-XXXX-A is put into effect. Alongside the salvaging and cremation of any leftover body parts stuck to SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Consider:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>then SCP-XXXX-A is to be terminated immediately, followed by the salvage and cremation of any appendages attached to SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Issues this is fixing:</p>
<ul>
<li>weird sentence fragment (&quot;Alongside&#8230;&quot;</li>
<li>'stuck' feeling really casual</li>
<li>just a real clunky way to say they are terminated.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>the following anomalies' effects will begin to take place.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'anomalous', not &quot;anomalies'&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXX-A’s skin, blood, flesh, and veins will begin to deteriorate into the curtain discarding only bones.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'leaving' would be clearer than 'discarding'; and 'transform' or 'become part of'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This will rapidly lead to blood loss shortly after.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this feels odd because like you just said that the blood gets transformed too so like&#8230;it feels on the one hand like the natural conclusion and on the other, like it's technically not actually blood loss in the conventional sense.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If enough human matter equal to SCP-XXXX’s current size and weight is consumed within a recorded one to two day period</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I recommended you cut enough last time and I still recommend that:having both 'enough' and 'equal to' is super janky since they're both referring to the quantity and they aren't synonymous.</p>
<p>I'd also cut 'recorded' here: the effects happen even if no-one is paying attention to record them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>from SCP-XXXX that includes: hair, multiple sets of eyes, legs</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'includes' should be 'include'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX has <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">noted to</span> adapt<span style="color: green">ed</span> to the situation it is currently faced with when consuming matter.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>stuff like 'noted to' isn't really necessary: you can just say what it does, not what people say it does</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When in this state <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">due to the adaptation of SCP-XXXX's body, it has been noted that</span> any of the <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">five</span> human senses can be enhanced.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Uh basically large parts of this sentence are just&#8230;.extraneous? All it's really saying is it can enhance it's senses and so that's all you need to say.</p>
<p>Also humans have, depending on how you count, between 9 and 21 senses: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sense#General">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sense#General</a> so it's probably best to just not specify the number.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Signs of SCP-XXXX first came to light when reports that an unknown shapeless entity had plummeted from the sky, right before the reported deaths of 225 people in Champagne Illinois on 7/27/2005 (Stated below).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'first came to light' feels weirdly casual here. You should also have a comma between 'Champagne' and 'Illinois'. I&#8230;.generally don't like this. It should be part of the discovery section but it also feels&#8230;kinda dumb that it came from space? I feel like it'd be more effective if it opened with the town going silent.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to three days of silence from 7/27/2005 - 7/30/2005, 225 reports of civilian deaths were noted by Champagne Illinois requesting military and police aid from the government, following the sighting of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>three days of silence doesn't smoothly flow into reports of civilian deaths: this needs some kinda intermediary thing where someone is sent to investigate and discovers them.</p>
<p>The requesting also doesn't flow because there's no-one mentioned who requests this or indication of there actually being a sighting of SCP-XXXX. This sentence is trying to do too much in too few words.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As an immediate response to this unusual jump and spike of numbers, the foundation took interest and shut out every entrance into this town.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What 'unusual jump and spike of numbers'? This should directly reference the reports, not vaguely refer to them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A priority was set that no one enters or leaves the surrounding area.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this isn't a &quot;priority&quot;. Consider: &quot;The surrounding area was locked down&quot; or &quot;The Foundation locked the town down to prevent anyone entering or leaving.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX was noted in the phase of growth and developing in the husk of a middle-aged man who still hasn’t been identified as of yet when recovered.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>another needless used of noted.</p>
<p>'as of yet when recovered' mixes two times together in a confusing way; I'd move 'when recovered' to the start maybe though it feels like the recovery is being elided in an unsatisfying way here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX was seen using this husk as a way to disguise and move around the town freely consuming matter, equivalent enough to produce limbs and perform its anomaly’s abilities.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'equivalent enough' is super janky and everything after it feels tacked on.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Commander Linwood ruled nothing could be done beyond the starvation of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why? At this point, it's almost certainly unclear that starving SCP-XXXX does anything.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and tipping its containment box that it was stored in over at random periods.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>why isn't the box heavy enough to prevent this or w/e? This feels like a bad containment procedure, particularly since there's ways to stop a box moving without attaching bolts (bolts are bad since they add holes from the outside to the inside of the box)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After two confirmed weeks of continued testing,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;confirmed&quot; is unnecessary here</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX was reportedly able to jump out and wrap itself around one of the researchers examining SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;reportedly&quot; adds unnecessary uncertainty about what happened here: either it did do this or it didn't but it can't be both.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This resulted in the researcher panicking and frantically rip SCP-XXXX off of them, but the suit was torn in the process</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'ripped' would be more grammatically correct.</p>
<p>&quot;the suit&quot; is unclear here because like&#8230;who's suit is it? the researchers? it's mentioned like it's been referred to previously but it hasn't so it feels jarring.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Little matter was consumed in the time it took to contain SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What does 'little matter' mean here?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The internal structure of the containment cell was slightly weakened and shortly repaired after the incident.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'but repaired shortly after the incident' would be clearer. Then again, it being damaged and then immediately repaired raises the question of why even bother mentioning this since it's so inconsequential?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Both Dr. Connor and other researchers working with SCP-XXXX have put into question the severity of another containment breach Scp be tied up with any bounded material to ensure SCP-XXXX does not breach containment by force. And to make sure future examinations undergo safely.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Awkward fragment sentence at the end; Dr. Connor is mentioned here like we're meant to know who he is but this is the first mention of it; and everything after 'containment breach' feels like an editing mistake?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>From recent research and developments that have been made about SCP-XXXX, it appears that SCP-XXXX does not only retain the form of a shower curtain but; also possess shapeshifting abilities.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is wack because we aren't shown any of the research or developments that lead to this conclusion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>During a recent on-site breach. (See Addendum Incident-2) SCP-XXXX accumulated enough time to take the shape and form of a researcher assigned to SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The first period here is unnecessary.</p>
<p>Also why wasn't this mentioned in the Incident-2 section since it happened then?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX has expressed several hostile concerns about returning home</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What is a 'hostile concern about returning home'?</p>
<p>Also like why are we not shown any of this until the interview? Like I don't see why you're summarising this all here and then immediately launching into an interview that like&#8230;reveals this?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And or lacks the nutrients to perform said anomalies effects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Weird sentence fragment. You do this <em>a lot</em>: if you are starting a sentence with a conjunction like 'and' or 'but', it probably wants to be part of the previous sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Interviewer: Dr. Conner</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Every other time, you've spelt this 'Connor'. Either is correct but it should be consistent.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(Dr. Connor is interrupted by SCP-XXXX)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unnecessary: &quot;founda-&quot; makes it clear Connor was interrupted, and the SCP talking next makes it clear it was by the SCP.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If it will humor you, then I’ll play your little games until I become bored.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this just feels really weird? one moment the SCP is demanding to be released, the next it's being passive</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Starvation doesn’t seem to have any effect either, other than keeping it to a containable and safe <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">enough</span> size.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd be tempted to cut 'and safe' here too. Also like&#8230;why? Surely if starvation is effective at weakening it, applying more starvation will, eventually, kill it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following a cataclysmic breach of Site-19. SCP-XXXX was reported to be</p>
</blockquote>
<p>these should be the same sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Both Dr. Adam and Dr. Connor’s body were recovered after the incident is declared deceased as of this time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I believe that 'is' should be 'and'.</p>
